message.content:
To expose runtime metrics to the client HUD in a protocol-first way while maintaining simulation authority in the runtime, we need to:

1.  **Extend the Protocol**: Add a new `runtime.metrics` server message type to `packages/protocol/src/messages.ts`.
2.  **Update Runtime Server**: Modify `apps/game-runtime/src/runtime/GameRuntimeServer.ts` to include metrics in the `session.state` payload and emit a dedicated `runtime.metrics` message when metrics change (or include them in the state update).
3.  **Update Client**: Modify `apps/game-client/src/App.tsx` to consume the new metrics and display them in the HUD.
4.  **Add Contract Tests**: Add tests to `apps/game-runtime/test/ui-contract.integration.test.ts` to verify the new message structure.

Here is the smallest safe fix set:

### 1. Extend Protocol (`packages/protocol/src/messages.ts`)

Add the `RuntimeMetrics` type and the `runtime.metrics` message variant.

```typescript
// packages/protocol/src/messages.ts

// ... existing imports ...

export type RuntimeMetrics = {
  turnsTotal: number;
  invalidOutputCount: number;
  actionAppliedCount: number;
  actionRejectedCount: number;
  queuedActions: number;
};

// ... existing types ...

export type ServerMessage =
  | {
      version: Version;
      type: "session.state";
      payload: {
        phase: SessionPhase;
        paused: boolean;
        preparedSeed: number | null;
        threadIds: { gameplayByAgentId: Record<string, string>; builder?: string };
        connected: boolean;
        runtime: {
          model: string | null;
          effort: TurnEffort;
          tickMs: number;
          schedulerMs: number;
          maxQueuedActionsBeforeTurn: number;
          availableModels: RuntimeModelOption[];
          metrics?: RuntimeMetrics; // Add metrics here for initial state
        };
      };
    }
  | {
      version: Version;
      type: "runtime.metrics"; // New message type
      payload: RuntimeMetrics;
    }
  | {
      version: Version;
      type: "world.snapshot";
      // ... existing world.snapshot payload ...
    }
  | {
      version: Version;
      type: "agent.turn";
      // ... existing agent.turn payload ...
    }
  | {
      version: Version;
      type: "agent.action";
      // ... existing agent.action payload ...
    }
  | {
      version: Version;
      type: "agent.feed";
      // ... existing agent.feed payload ...
    }
  | {
      version: Version;
      type: "build.result";
      // ... existing build.result payload ...
    }
  | {
      version: Version;
      type: "error";
      // ... existing error payload ...
    };

// ... existing schemas ...
```

### 2. Update Runtime Server (`apps/game-runtime/src/runtime/GameRuntimeServer.ts`)

Update `sendSessionState` to include metrics and emit `runtime.metrics` when they change.

```typescript
// apps/game-runtime/src/runtime/GameRuntimeServer.ts

// ... existing imports ...

// ... inside GameRuntimeServer class ...

  private sendSessionState(): void {
    this.emitMessage({
      version: PROTOCOL_VERSION,
      type: "session.state",
      payload: {
        phase: this.phase,
        paused: this.paused,
        preparedSeed: this.preparedSeed,
        threadIds: this.threadIds,
        connected: this.connected,
        runtime: {
          model: this.turnModel,
          effort: this.turnEffort,
          tickMs: runtimeConfig.tickMs,
          schedulerMs: runtimeConfig.schedulerMs,
          maxQueuedActionsBeforeTurn: runtimeConfig.maxQueuedActionsBeforeTurn,
          availableModels: this.availableModels,
          metrics: this.metrics.snapshot() // Include current metrics
        }
      }
    });
  }

  // Add a helper to emit metrics updates
  private emitMetricsUpdate(): void {
    this.emitMessage({
      version: PROTOCOL_VERSION,
      type: "runtime.metrics",
      payload: this.metrics.snapshot()
    });
  }

  // Update methods that change metrics to also emit the update
  public onTurnCompleted(): void {
    this.metrics.onTurnCompleted();
    this.emitMetricsUpdate();
  }

  public onInvalidOutput(): void {
    this.metrics.onInvalidOutput();
    this.emitMetricsUpdate();
  }

  public onActionApplied(): void {
    this.metrics.onActionApplied();
    this.emitMetricsUpdate();
  }

  public onActionRejected(): void {
    this.metrics.onActionRejected();
    this.emitMetricsUpdate();
  }

  public setQueueDepth(depth: number): void {
    this.metrics.setQueueDepth(depth);
    this.emitMetricsUpdate();
  }

  // ... rest of the class ...
```

*Note: Since `MetricsTracker` is a separate class, we need to either pass a callback to it or call `emitMetricsUpdate` from the `GameRuntimeServer` methods that call the tracker. The cleanest approach without refactoring `MetricsTracker` is to call `emitMetricsUpdate` immediately after calling the tracker methods in `GameRuntimeServer`.*

